.TH "LMM" "1" "Nov 2026" "lmm 2.3.0" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
lmm-profile-sync - Sync profile to match installed mods


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBlmm profile sync [name] [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Update the profile YAML to match currently installed/enabled mods in the database.

.PP
Enabled mods the profile does not list are added, and entries for mods not
installed under the profile at all are removed. An entry for a mod that is
installed but switched off is always kept, with its load-order position and
pinned version: on the active profile a warning says when the database and
the file disagree about it.

.PP
Use this if the profile got out of sync, or to migrate from pre-profile installs.
If no name is given, uses the current/default profile. Prompts for
confirmation before making any changes; pass -y/--yes to skip the prompt.

.PP
With --collection, the sync updates a collection profile from its origin
instead: a profile imported from an https address fetches it again and
updates when the document is the same collection (its id) at a newer
version; one imported from a NexusMods collection updates to its newest
revision. The plan lists the mods the new version adds, the ones whose pin
it moves (a mod the collection brought in follows it, locked or not), the
ones it no longer lists, and conflicts: mods you changed yourself (locked,
unlocked, updated, rolled back, updated from a file, re-answered the
installer of, or re-linked) that it changes. A mod the new version drops is kept, as your own, unless
you name it with --remove (or pass --remove-obsolete); a conflict keeps
your version unless you name it with --override (or pass
--override-conflicts). The plugin list and config overrides follow the
same rule: what you changed since the collection last applied them stays
yours, and where you both changed something the plan names the conflict
and its --override key (plugin:<name>, plugins:order, plugins:rules,
override:<path>). The new version passes the same checks an import
does: features this lmm lacks refuse the update, its hooks are listed and
never applied, and overrides that are not text config files are held back
without --allow-any-overrides. --dry-run --json prints the plan.

.PP
Examples:
  lmm profile sync --game skyrim-se
  lmm profile sync survival --game skyrim-se
  lmm profile sync survival --game skyrim-se --yes
  lmm profile sync survival --game skyrim-se --collection --dry-run
  lmm profile sync survival --game skyrim-se --collection --remove-obsolete --override 3863


.SH OPTIONS
\fB--allow-any-overrides\fP[=false]
	with --collection, import the new version's overrides that are not text config files too

.PP
\fB--collection\fP[=false]
	update a collection profile from its origin: the address it was imported from, or its NexusMods collection's newest revision

.PP
\fB--dry-run\fP[=false]
	print what the sync would do without changing anything

.PP
\fB--fomod-choices\fP=""
	answer FOMOD installer questions from a JSON file (the profile's fomod: shape or Vortex's choices array; key it by source:id when several mods ask, or by source:id#file-id for one file's installer)

.PP
\fB--fomod-defaults\fP[=false]
	answer FOMOD installer questions with the installer's own preselection

.PP
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for sync

.PP
\fB--optional\fP=[]
	with --collection, install this optional item of a NexusMods collection (its id as the plan lists it, mod-id:file-id; repeatable)

.PP
\fB--override\fP=[]
	with --collection, take the collection's side of this conflict: a mod you changed (source:mod-id, or the mod id), plugin:<name>, plugins:order, plugins:rules or override:<path>, as the plan names it; repeatable

.PP
\fB--override-conflicts\fP[=false]
	with --collection, take the collection's side of every conflict - mods, plugin list and overrides (yours is kept by default)

.PP
\fB--remove\fP=[]
	with --collection, remove this mod the new version drops (source:mod-id, or the mod id; repeatable)

.PP
\fB--remove-obsolete\fP[=false]
	with --collection, remove every mod the new version drops (they are kept by default)

.PP
\fB--with-optional\fP[=false]
	with --collection, install a NexusMods collection's optional mods too

.PP
\fB-y\fP, \fB--yes\fP[=false]
	auto-confirm the sync


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--config\fP=""
	config directory (default: $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/lmm or ~/.config/lmm)

.PP
\fB--data\fP=""
	data directory (default: $XDG_DATA_HOME/lmm or ~/.local/share/lmm)

.PP
\fB-g\fP, \fB--game\fP=""
	game ID to operate on

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	output JSON instead of text; mutating commands print their result, --dry-run prints the plan; never prompts

.PP
\fB--log-level\fP="off"
	diagnostic log level written to stderr (off, error, warn, info, debug)

.PP
\fB--no-color\fP[=false]
	disable colored output (NO_COLOR env is also honored)

.PP
\fB--no-hooks\fP[=false]
	disable all hooks

.PP
\fB-v\fP, \fB--verbose\fP[=false]
	verbose output


